Now I understand the full kernel. Here's my insight for a shfl-based rewrite that eliminates BOTH syncwarps and the smem round-trip:

The critical issue: `__shfl_sync` is warp-collective — ALL lanes (including lane k and inactive lanes) must execute it. The current code has the broadcast inside `if(lane==k)` (only lane k) then syncwarp. To use shfl, I lift the broadcast OUT of the `if`.

New per-step structure (no smem, no syncwarp):
```
// lane k computes tau_k, beta, scale into a per-lane var (only meaningful on lane k)
float tau_k=0, beta=0, scale=1;
if (lane==k) { ... compute alpha, tail2, beta, tau_k, scale; taub[k]=tau_k; colreg[k]=beta; }
// broadcast tau_k and scale from lane k to all lanes (collective)
tau_k = __shfl_sync(FULL, tau_k, k);
scale = __shfl_sync(FULL, scale, k);
// lane k finalizes its v into colreg[r>k] *= scale (so colreg[r] = v[r])
if (lane==k) { for r>k: colreg[r] *= scale; }
// Now broadcast v[r] (=colreg[r] on lane k) for the apply.
// Each consumer lane>k computes w = colreg[k] + sum_{r>k} v[r]*colreg[r]
float w = colreg[k];
#pragma unroll
for (int r=0; r<NMAX; ++r) {
    float vr = __shfl_sync(FULL, colreg[r], k);  // v[r] from lane k (collective)
    if (r>k && r<n && lane>k && lane<n) w += vr*colreg[r];
    // also stash vr for the update loop... but shfl result differs per r
}
```

Problem: the apply needs v[r] twice (once for w, once for the update `colreg[r] -= v[r]*tw`). And `tw` depends on `w` which needs ALL v[r] first. So I need two passes over r, each needing v[r] broadcast. That's 64 shfls per step (32 for w-accumulation, 32 for update) — but shfls are collective and cheap (~1 cycle issue). 

Alternatively, broadcast all v[r] into a local register array `vloc[NMAX]` once (32 shfls), then both passes read vloc from registers. That's 32 shfls + 2 register-passes. The vloc array adds 32 registers/thread (pushing past 88 — might spill). Hmm.

Actually — let me reconsider the cost-benefit. The kernel is 48us at 32 steps = 1.5us/step. The smem round-trip per step is: lane k writes ~32 smem floats, syncwarp, all lanes read ~32 smem floats in a dependent loop. At 3% occupancy the syncwarp + smem latency isn't hidden. Replacing with shfl removes the syncwarp barriers (2 per step) which at low occupancy are pure stall.

